<article class="prose explainer-page">
  <header><p class="eyebrow">A calmer way to discover</p><h1>How OnMyRadar works</h1><p class="lede">Describe what matters once. Your Radar watches approved event sources and brings back bounded, explainable results.</p></header>
  <ol class="numbered-cards">
    <li><h2>Describe your Radar</h2><p>Use natural language to describe topics, timing, distance, budget, who the event is for, and what should be excluded. Review the interpreted criteria before saving.</p></li>
    <li><h2>Review current matches</h2><p>OnMyRadar checks approved sources, combines duplicates, and filters by your explicit criteria before ranking. Each visible event shows source attribution, freshness, and reasons for the match.</p></li>
    <li><h2>Choose what happens next</h2><p><strong>Save event</strong> bookmarks it. <strong>I’m interested</strong> improves relevance. <strong>Not for me</strong> suppresses that match. <strong>Tune this Radar</strong> changes the Radar only after you confirm the edit.</p></li>
  </ol>
  <section><h2>List and map, one result set</h2><p>Explore provides a list and a map view of the same bounded results. Map searches happen only after you choose “Search this area.” Location is optional, foreground-only, and approximate; you can select an area instead.</p></section>
  <section><h2>Know what OnMyRadar knows</h2><p>Events can change or be wrong. The app distinguishes fresh, stale, changed, cancelled, and unavailable information and links to the original source for verification. A match is a recommendation—not a ticket, reservation, or promise that an event will happen.</p></section>
  <section class="callout"><h2>You remain in control</h2><p>Pause or delete Radars, change notification preferences, report an inaccurate event, export your data, or delete your account from the app.</p><p><a class="text-link" href="{{ '/privacy/' | url }}">Read the Privacy Policy</a></p></section>
</article>
